00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.350
We just abstract all
that away for you.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:03.850
And we see examples of this.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.090
We've got customers
on Cloud Functions,

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:10.380
on Google App Engine that are
serving millions of end users

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.730
with ridiculously small
operations teams of, like, one

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:14.800
or two people.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.130
And so that just shows
you how productive,

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.860
how lean a developer team
can be when it doesn't have

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.400
to think about infrastructure.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.120
And so I'm super excited about
broadening that, so products

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.520
like Cloud Run, that lets
you bring many, many more

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.340
things to serverless--
so that all developers

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:36.228
can reap those benefits of
serverless architecture.

00:02:36.228 --> 00:02:37.770
KAROLINA NETOLICKA:
So one thing that

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.960
excites me about serverless is
you only pay for what you use.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.520
And so I'll tell you a story.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.645
I joined the App Engine
team about three years ago,

00:02:44.645 --> 00:02:47.103
and so, I've worked on a number
of features for App Engine.

00:02:47.103 --> 00:02:49.260
And, typically, when
we're close to releasing

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.350
I create a personal test
account with my personal email

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:56.160
because I want to just make sure
to use it like a regular user.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.920
And I test the feature,
and then we ship it.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.040
And so recently, I started
working on VPC connectivity.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.100
And so, I created a
personal test project,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.680
and I created a
couple VMs, so that I

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.160
have something to connect to.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.960
And I sent out the link
to a couple other people.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.760
And at the end of the
month, I got a bill,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.790
and it was $250 to
my personal account.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:18.840
And I said, what is this?

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.465
I've never gotten a
bill in three years

00:03:20.465 --> 00:03:22.860
that I've worked on App Engine
for testing these things.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.010
And then I realized,
still, serverless is great.

00:03:27.125 --> 00:03:29.250
MICHAEL ABD-EL-MALEK: What
I'm really excited about

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.070
is just how easy it is
to build applications,

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:36.630
and serverless has got more
powerful over the years, right?

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:39.000
We have a bunch of
limitations, especially

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.610
in the earlier
versions of App Engine.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:44.130
And say, Cloud Functions--
we had a concurrency limit,

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:48.540
and there were a fixed number of
languages that were expanding,

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.240
but there's still some
kind of limitations there.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:56.130
But we're now getting into a
much, much lower limitations

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:58.650
in Cloud Run and
that just makes it

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.990
that there's a wider
room of applications

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.048
you can develop, while
still getting the benefits

00:04:03.048 --> 00:04:04.590
that my colleagues
earlier mentioned,

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.360
in terms of not having to think
at all about any operations

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.880
aspect.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.490
MATTHEW SOLDO: Yeah, so you
know, it's almost become trite,

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:16.450
but it really is the case
that every business is

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:18.589
becoming a software company.

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:21.730
And I really think for the
majority of businesses,

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:23.980
serverless is the right
solution for them.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:27.610
Unless you are innovating at
a very low level of compute,

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:31.000
why bother dealing
with servers or OSes.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.660
I was talking to a customer
yesterday, who's on App Engine,

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:38.260
and they are a book
self-publishing business.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.790
They let authors
self-publish their books,

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:43.420
and they have a three-person
development team.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.550
Very, very small, and
they're able to do

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:48.100
that because of serverless
and the productivity

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:49.600
that it provides them.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.310
And enabling
businesses like that

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.932
really is what gets me
excited and makes me

00:04:53.932 --> 00:04:55.390
psyched to go to
bed today and shop

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:57.973
to work and make it easier
for those kinds of companies.

00:04:57.973 --> 00:05:00.140
DONNA MALAYERI: Yeah, for
me it's something similar.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.557
I've worked in the serverless
space for over three years--

00:05:02.557 --> 00:05:03.820
not all of it at Google--

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.730
and I've worked in programming
languages and developer tools

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.020
my entire career.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.380
And the big aha moment for me
about why serverless was great

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:14.200
is when I had a lot of client
developers come up to me

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.900
and say, I have never
built an application that

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:18.160
connects to something before.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:18.910
Not end-to-end.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:20.620
I was always writing
the client, someone else

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:21.620
was writing the service.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.980
And I have never done
anything on the server before.

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:26.770
And I walked up, and I
used a Functions product,

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:28.420
and now I'm productive.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.400
Now I can build an
end-to-end application.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.250
I can work on the parts I
know, which is the client.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.620
And the server
stuff is so simple

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.390
that I can manage
that all myself.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.040
So it really eases
the burden of having

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:41.290
to do something on the server.

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:44.650
It makes it a lot more
accessible for more developers.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:48.250
And even for developers who
could build a complex server

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.190
application, you don't have to.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:51.940
You get to focus
on the fun stuff,

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:55.090
as they were saying in the
developer keynote yesterday.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.110
All right, so I'm
going to open--

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:59.090
I'm going to ask
some panel questions,

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:00.670
and then we'll get
to Q&amp;A. So we're

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:03.410
going to do about
maybe 15 minutes

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.110
of pre-canned
questions, and then

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:07.750
we'll take questions from you.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:11.110
So Morgan, this week,
we launched Cloud Run.

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.840
Can you tell us what it
is, and how is it different

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:17.100
from Cloud Functions?

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.090
MORGAN HALLMON: Sure.

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.520
So Cloud Run, you've
probably heard about it.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.078
It was in, I think, two or
three keynotes, actually.

00:06:23.078 --> 00:06:24.870
DONNA MALAYERI: Actually,
a show of hands--

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:29.500
who has seen Cloud
Run demos this week?

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:30.698
Awesome.

00:06:30.698 --> 00:06:32.740
MORGAN HALLMON: For those
of you who haven't, you

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:34.680
know, watch Michael's talk.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.320
Very impressive.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.210
Sp, Cloud Run.

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:40.380
So what it is is a
product that allows

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.890
you to run
HTTP-driven containers

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:45.432
in a serverless fashion.

00:06:45.432 --> 00:06:46.390
So what does that mean?

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:47.973
It means if you can
build a container,

00:06:47.973 --> 00:06:50.098
if you can hit the
HTTP, you can run it

00:06:50.098 --> 00:06:52.140
without worrying about
any of the infrastructure.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.180
So all the provisioning
of the infrastructure,

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.670
all the configuration,
all the scaling--

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:57.870
Google worries about that.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.590
You worry about your
code and your container.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:05.610
And so, that probably seems
very interesting but why

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:06.340
is it important?

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:08.550
It's important
because, historically,

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:12.960
one of the biggest barriers to
adoption of services for some

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.510
of our other products, like
Google App Engine or Cloud

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:19.800
Functions, has
been that customers

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.540
haven't had as much control as
they needed over the runtime.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:26.700
Now let's be honest, App Engine
and Functions support a ton

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.170
of different languages
and runtimes, so many,

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:33.370
many things fit very neatly
into the serverless box.

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:34.440
But if your app didn't--

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.930
and the worst case was when
you had one tiny, little thing,

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:40.500
like I need one library that I
just can't use with App Engine

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.050
or with Functions-- then
you were completely shut out

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:44.377
of the serverless vision.

00:07:44.377 --> 00:07:45.960
And so the great
thing about Cloud Run

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:48.300
is that because you
can bring in container,

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.250
you can basically
run anything, right?

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:53.310
So that key barrier has
now been broken down,

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:55.380
and we've broadened
the set of workloads

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:57.690
that are addressable by
serverless which really, really

00:07:57.690 --> 00:07:59.580
excites me.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:06.840
Cloud Functions is a much more
curated and opinionated product

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.730
in that we, Google,
provide you with runtime.

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:12.720
Now again, we support
many, many runtime,

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.400
many, many languages-- we've
got Java, Go, Python, Node.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.890
So many developers
can use Functions.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:22.590
It's not a huge limitation.

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:24.270
Obviously, as I said
before, Cloud Run

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:25.500
lifts this limitation.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.710
But if your thing does fit,
if your workload does fit

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:31.830
into a Cloud Functions
runtime, then it's

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:34.257
a much more productive,
much faster way

00:08:34.257 --> 00:08:36.299
of developing because you
just worry about what's

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:37.591
between those two curly braces.

00:08:37.591 --> 00:08:40.860
You just worry about the
business logic, the application

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.397
logic, and you leave
everything else to Google.

00:08:43.397 --> 00:08:45.480
So I'd say that's the big
difference between Cloud

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.670
Run and Cloud Functions.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:51.210
I do want to talk about the
Functions Framework, which

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:53.340
we also announced
a couple days ago.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.140
So the Functions
Framework is it's kind

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.170
of like the best of both worlds,
in a sense, in that it allows

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.260
you to write function
code-- so again,

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:04.470
just worrying about what's
between those two curly braces,

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:06.670
not worrying about
any of the rest.

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:10.620
But however, run it anywhere
and be very, very portable

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.680
in that the Functions
Framework is, essentially,

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.200
a library or set
of files that you

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:19.380
would put in to your directory
in what you're developing.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:22.110
And it takes care of all
the configuration that's

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:26.900
needed to build that container
that can then run Cloud Run,

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:30.270
can then run Cloud Run on
GKE, can then run anywhere

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:34.530
that Knative, which is the open
API specification for Cloud

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:35.880
Run, run.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:39.270
So you can run on any Kubernetes
cluster running Knative.

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:41.150
So it could be on-prem,
it could be on AWS,

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:43.585
it could be on Azure,
wherever you want to run that.

00:09:43.585 --> 00:09:45.960
So it's kind of the best of
both worlds, in that you just

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:47.818
focus on the application
logic, yet you

00:09:47.818 --> 00:09:49.860
get the portability and
the flexibility of having

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.260
a container that runs anywhere.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:53.250
DONNA MALAYERI: Great.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:56.843
All right, so, Morgan,
you mentioned that Michael

00:09:56.843 --> 00:09:58.010
had a great talk about this.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:09:59.730
He did a scale
demo that everybody

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:02.330
is talking about on Twitter--

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:06.052
where you scaled up to, I
think it was, how many nodes?

00:10:06.052 --> 00:10:07.260
MICHAEL ABD-EL-MALEK: 10,000.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:10.000
DONNA MALAYERI: 10,000 nodes
in less than 10 seconds.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.180
So why don't you talk about
how we did this scaling,

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.210
and what kind of scale
we're seeing here

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:17.985
in Cloud Run that is really
unparalleled in this industry?

00:10:17.985 --> 00:10:19.110
MICHAEL ABD-EL-MALEK: Sure.

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:23.160
So with serverless workflows,
they tend to be bursty.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:27.240
And we wanted customers to
be able to use our serverless

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.940
products and not
have to worry about,

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:32.640
do I need to pre-spin instances?

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.780
Do I need to send some
artificial workload

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:40.080
to kind of get more instances
ready for any kind of event

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:41.550
that I know is coming up?

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:43.260
And so we wanted to
really kind of push

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:46.020
the boundaries on
rapid, fast scaling

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:48.810
of instances, based
on customer workloads

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:50.580
that customers
don't have to think

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:55.650
about any capacity or instances,
pre-warming, et cetera.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:58.860
And the way we've kind of pushed
the boundary in Cloud Run--

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:01.800
and I just want to note that the
same infrastructure powers all

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.470
of our three
serverless products,

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:07.260
and so we've kind of enabling
it first in Cloud Run,

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:10.110
but we'll get it soon
in Cloud Functions.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:13.552
And App Engine's billing model
makes this a bit difficult

00:11:13.552 --> 00:11:15.010
to introduce there,
but we're going

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:17.130
to have this in Cloud
Functions as well.

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:20.090
But to Donna's
question, so how do we

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:24.520
scale to 10,000 instances in
a matter of 5, 10 seconds?

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:27.020
The way we do this is when a
request comes to our networking

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:30.350
front ends, the front ends
go through a small set

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:32.240
of application servers
to see if there's

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.150
an available instance that
can handle that request

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:38.390
or if these application servers
can start a new instance

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:40.110
to handle their request.

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:43.310
Now, if the application servers
are busy because they already

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:46.450
have too many instances,
the networking front ends

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:49.910
will try an ever-expanding
group of application servers

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:52.430
and start an instance in order
to look for an instance that

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:54.590
has available capacity.

00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:56.630
And kind of the key
thing that makes

00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:01.040
this work is that all the
different networking front

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:02.540
ends, when we have
thousands of them

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:06.140
in each region in a
decentralized managed manner,

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:08.240
use consistent hashing,
so that they're

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:10.910
going through the same
set of application servers

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:15.900
for a particular service and
revision as the other front

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:17.690
ends without any communication.

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:20.750
So there's no centralized
scheduling or centralized load

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:23.540
balancer that can either
be a bottleneck for scaling

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:25.920
or an availability bottleneck.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:31.340
And so, as we try bigger and
bigger sets of application

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:34.610
servers to start instances,
that kind of simultaneously

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:38.480
lets us scale quickly but also
avoid too many cold starts.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:41.630
Because you could-- one
way to do rapid scaling

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:44.323
is to just randomly go to
any application server.

00:12:44.323 --> 00:12:45.740
And if you have
thousands of them,

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:47.532
you're going to incur
a lot of cold starts,

00:12:47.532 --> 00:12:48.600
more than you need.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:52.470
And so as we kind of go
an ever-expanding groups,

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.200
and as long as
application servers can

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:55.660
respond quickly
and say, hey, I'm

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:58.430
busy go to another
application server,

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:00.530
then we can rapidly scale
in a matter of seconds

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:02.023
to thousands of instances.

00:13:02.023 --> 00:13:04.190
DONNA MALAYERI: Right, and
the reason this matters--

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:05.648
and cold start is
the time it takes

00:13:05.648 --> 00:13:08.400
when there's nothing running
to actually getting a response,

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:08.900
right?

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:11.240
And so you want customers to
get a response right away,

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:13.190
so you want low cold start.

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:15.590
And before serverless and
before this fast scale-out,

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:17.510
what customers would do--

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:19.370
and in other cloud
vendors I worked in,

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:20.562
they would pre-provision.

00:13:20.562 --> 00:13:22.520
They're like, oh, we have
a big sale coming up,

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:23.703
or it's Black Friday.

00:13:23.703 --> 00:13:26.120
Let's allocate-- and I'm seeing
some nods in the audience.

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:26.870
You know.

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:31.220
Let's allocate thousands of
VMs in advance so we're ready.

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:33.590
And what happens is you
have to do all this capacity

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:34.580
planning in advance.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:36.980
You pay for capacity
you're not using.

00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:39.680
If you're wrong, if
you've under-provisioned,

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:43.010
now you're really stuck
because you have to wait,

00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:46.070
you know, minutes for
new capacity to start up.

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:48.590
That's why the scale-out
is so valuable,

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:51.530
and it's one of those things
that makes serverless actually

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:53.720
useful in practice, when
you have a lot of load

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:54.830
coming to your service.

00:13:54.830 --> 00:13:57.470
So I recommend watching the
video for Michael's talk.

00:13:57.470 --> 00:13:59.850
It's really fascinating.

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:01.225
So now, we mentioned
the product.

00:14:01.225 --> 00:14:03.017
And of course, when a
new product launches,

00:14:03.017 --> 00:14:05.270
everyone asks, does this
mean you're not working

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:06.440
on the old products anymore?

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:07.880
And the answer is
no, we are still

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:09.390
working on the old products.

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:11.960
And if you look at our
blog post, you can see,

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:13.490
we have a lot of
new improvements

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:16.220
in Cloud Functions,
as well as App Engine.

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:18.200
Matt is the product
manager for App Engine.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.360
So why don't you tell us
about what we've released

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:22.130
for App Engine this week?

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:23.547
MATTHEW SOLDO:
Yeah, that's right.

00:14:23.547 --> 00:14:26.150
So we got a whole
paragraph in the blog post

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:30.020
about our serverless offerings,
but, really, development

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:31.420
is very active on App Engine.

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:33.410
We release a lot of
really fun new things.

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:37.772
So some of you may see--
who is an App Engine user,

00:14:37.772 --> 00:14:39.230
is running something
on App Engine?

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.000
Good, about a quarter
of the audience.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:41.510
Great.

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:44.910
So last year, we released
second-generation runtimes

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:46.070
for App Engine.

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:48.020
These are built on the
same infrastructure

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:49.365
that Cloud Run is built on.

00:14:49.365 --> 00:14:51.740
Under the covers, they run,
actually, a Docker container,

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:53.450
but you're not exposed to that.

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:58.250
And these unshackled App Engine
from the previous constraints,

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:01.010
where you can run
any language library.

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:03.590
You have open network access
which, believe it or not,

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:06.530
you did not have in the
first edition of App Engine.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:10.073
And so basically,
you can run any code.

00:15:10.073 --> 00:15:11.990
You don't have to write
your code specifically

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:14.300
for App Engine, which
you did in the past.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:19.760
And so on Tuesday, we
announced several new runtimes.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:25.130
We have PHP 7.2 in GA,
Python 3.7 in GA, Node 10,

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:31.400
and we also have Ruby in
alpha and Java 11 in alpha.

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:34.680
It didn't make the blog
post, but right about now,

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:39.540
or shortly, Go 1.12 is
also going into beta.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:42.170
So we have a slew
of new languages.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:43.790
It's really awesome.

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:45.973
But almost more
importantly-- and Karolina's

00:15:45.973 --> 00:15:47.390
going to talk about
more of this--

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:51.260
is VPC Connect for
App Engine, where

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:53.540
you can have an App
Engine app connect

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:56.250
to services that are running
in your Google Cloud VPC.

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:57.740
So yeah, that's right.

00:15:57.740 --> 00:15:59.210
That's right.

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:04.220
So the biggest requests we
had on the second-generation

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:08.900
runtimes was the ability
to access a cache

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:10.550
because the second-gen
runtimes do not

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:13.130
use the legacy App
Engine services,

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:16.070
like the Memcache service that
was bundled with App Engine

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:16.850
originally.

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:18.130
So now, it's really easy.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:19.910
You spin up a VPC connector.

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:22.520
You spin up your Redis
and Cloud Memorystore,

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:23.980
and you've got an awesome cache.

00:16:23.980 --> 00:16:25.340
It's plain vanilla Redis.

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:27.080
It works great.

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:31.760
One final thing that
is really exciting

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:34.820
that also didn't make the
blog post, but many of you'll

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:37.550
be hearing about this for the
first time, is this quarter,

00:16:37.550 --> 00:16:39.350
we're going to be
doubling the memory

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:42.162
on all second-generation
runtimes at the same price.

00:16:42.162 --> 00:16:44.120
So whatever the memory
is on the instances now,

00:16:44.120 --> 00:16:46.770
you'll be getting double
that, no change in price.

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:48.060
So really exciting.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:49.550
It's a great product.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.500
We've designed the
second-generation runtimes

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:54.540
to be future-compatible
with Cloud Run,

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:57.470
so if you are shy about
using a beta product--

00:16:57.470 --> 00:16:58.260
no problem.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:16:59.780
Develop on App Engine.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:02.233
And then it's going to
be an incredibly simple,

00:17:02.233 --> 00:17:04.400
like almost a one-button
migration, if you ever want

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:06.202
to use Cloud Run in the future.

00:17:06.202 --> 00:17:07.119
DONNA MALAYERI: Right.

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:09.750
Yeah, everyone got really
excited about VPC connectors.

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:11.810
This is a really
oft request feature.

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:13.220
We finally made it happen.

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:16.670
We can-- if you want to apply
VPC connectors, go ahead now.

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:20.869
And Karolina is the one
who made this happen.

00:17:20.869 --> 00:17:23.637
So why don't you tell us
about the feature we launched,

00:17:23.637 --> 00:17:25.220
and why it's important
beyond the case

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:26.869
that Matt just talked about?

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:28.119
KAROLINA NETOLICKA: All right.

00:17:28.119 --> 00:17:30.140
So if you use App
Engine, chances

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:33.390
are most customers use more
than one of our Cloud products,

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:33.890
right?

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:37.550
So there are many use cases
where you actually you

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:40.060
want to use App Engine
or Cloud Functions-- so

00:17:40.060 --> 00:17:42.500
serverless front end basically.

00:17:42.500 --> 00:17:44.540
But it needs to
talk to something

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:45.740
that's running in your VPC.

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:49.610
Perhaps you have a
GCE VM that's running

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:51.202
some off-the-shelf
software that isn't

00:17:51.202 --> 00:17:52.910
a good match for
serverless, and you need

00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:55.260
to call that from App Engine.

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:56.190
Or perhaps you run--

00:17:56.190 --> 00:18:00.110
you wrote some services in GKE
because you needed to use GPUs,

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:02.660
and you need to access
that from Cloud Functions.

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:05.240
Or perhaps you have
an on-premise database

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:08.450
that's connected to your
VPC network via Cloud VPN,

00:18:08.450 --> 00:18:11.360
and you want to access that
from your App Engine app

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:13.520
as your storage layer.

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:15.890
So for all these use
cases, in the past,

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:17.150
the options were limited.

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:20.990
You had to basically give the
resource in your VPC network

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:25.680
a public IP address, and
that's how you connected to it.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:27.020
It's as if it was running--

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:29.300
like there was no
advantage to the fact

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:32.570
that they were both
running in Google Cloud.

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:35.420
So what we have
launched now is we

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:38.960
have a feature called
Serverless VPC Access,

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:41.270
and it's now in beta.

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:43.280
The way you use this
is you create a VPC

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:48.010
connector in the same
region as your app

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:51.340
and in the same VPC as the
things you're trying to access.

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:55.760
And then you redeploy your
app or function, so just

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:59.680
a new version, specifying
the name of the connector.

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:02.150
And from then on,
if in your code,

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:06.830
you call or make a
request to a private IP,

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:09.650
that request is going to
go to the VPC connector.

00:19:09.650 --> 00:19:13.640
And, from there,
travel through the VPC

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:16.310
according to however the VPC
network is already set up.

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:18.980
So it's going to respect
all of your firewall rules.

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:22.190
It's going to work
with Cloud VPN,

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:24.420
and it's going to
reach its destination.

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:28.500
So now, you can use Cloud
Memorystore, which, previously,

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:30.140
you actually couldn't
use because Cloud

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:33.200
Memorystore doesn't let
you have public IPs.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:37.100
And you can use Cloud
Functions and App Engine

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:42.620
with Compute Engine,
GKE, without having

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:46.680
to make those resources public,
so it improves your security.

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:48.180
It also lowers
latency a little bit.

00:19:50.868 --> 00:19:52.160
MATTHEW SOLDO: It's super cool.

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:53.090
DONNA MALAYERI: It's
a great feature.

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:53.690
Try it out.

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:55.440
We're all really,
really excited about it.

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:58.140
Unblocks lots of scenarios.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:01.790
So you know, there's two
versions of Cloud Run.

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:03.950
There's a Cloud Run hosted,
fully-managed version,

00:20:03.950 --> 00:20:05.510
and there's a Cloud Run on GKE.

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:08.660
And it's basically the same
developer experience on either,

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:11.960
but if you run in
the GKE version,

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:14.090
you're managing the
Kubernetes cluster yourself.

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:16.010
If you're running
on hosted Cloud Run,

00:20:16.010 --> 00:20:17.420
we manage things for you.

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:19.070
So Michael, can you
tell us what do we

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:20.690
manage on behalf of customers?

00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:23.090
What worry do we take
away from customers?

00:20:23.090 --> 00:20:24.920
MICHAEL ABD-EL-MALEK: Sure.

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:26.270
So I mean, GKE--

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:29.640
we are managing some of
the operations for you,

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:32.960
but you still have to
have a GKE cluster.

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:35.450
And so, with the
hosted Cloud Run,

00:20:35.450 --> 00:20:37.290
you don't have to have
a GKE cluster at all.

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:40.390
Or if you have a GKE
cluster for other use cases,

00:20:40.390 --> 00:20:43.760
but you don't want to kind
of mix those workloads,

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:49.010
that remains on our
internal hosted platform.

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:54.330
We can scale up faster on our
internal version of Cloud Run.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:57.870
And so that's zero to
10k kind of scaling demo

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:01.150
that Donna mentioned earlier--

00:21:01.150 --> 00:21:03.070
we do not scale that
fast yet on GKE,

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:06.170
so you wouldn't get that
scaling performance.

00:21:06.170 --> 00:21:08.570
And so, that's kind
of another thing

00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:11.660
that is an advantage of
using the hosted version.

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:15.100
Of course, we are working on
making GKE scalability faster,

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:17.770
both for this use case,
for Cloud Run on GKE,

00:21:17.770 --> 00:21:21.250
as well as other customer
use cases on GKE.

00:21:21.250 --> 00:21:22.720
But the kind of
technology that's

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:26.260
underlying Cloud Run
hosted has been easier

00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:29.180
to make it scale up faster.

00:21:29.180 --> 00:21:34.180
You know, there's
no exposed kind

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:35.680
of versions you
have to think about,

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:39.220
in terms of node versions
or GKE versions, master

00:21:39.220 --> 00:21:40.040
or node versions.

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:42.220
And so in the Cloud Run
hosted, there really

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:44.920
is no operational aspect
that you have to think about.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:46.410
All of that stuff is behind.

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:55.450
It runs internally in Google,
so all of the kind of security

00:21:55.450 --> 00:21:58.630
operating system image stuff--
all of that is hidden from you,

00:21:58.630 --> 00:22:00.570
and we take care of doing that.

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:02.410
If we have to update
our own servers,

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:04.480
that is done
transparently to you.

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:07.660
The request routing-- we'll find
and have an available version.

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:10.210
If there's any server failures,
node failures-- all of that

00:22:10.210 --> 00:22:11.237
is hidden from you.

00:22:11.237 --> 00:22:12.320
DONNA MALAYERI: All right.

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:13.210
Thank you very much.

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:14.585
Thank you for all
your questions.

00:22:14.585 --> 00:22:15.310
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:19.860
